ral government  that is ultimately responsible for  getting the vaccines  and providing that key information to  the provinces and increasing  sore point with the premiers donna  okay mercedes in ottawa thank you  the latest blow still ahead the pandemic  pushes hudson's bay to close  some stores  a company older than canada itself is  facing an uncertain future  hudson's bay like so many retailers has  been struggling during this pandemic  it is so bad the company has stopped  paying rent on several of its properties  as eric sorenson explains this year more  than ever holiday shopping will be  crucial  to survival the window displays in  downtown toronto have tickled the  spirits of christmas shoppers for  decades  and new ads with the stars of  award-winning shit's creek suggest  tis the season for hudson's bay to make  up for lost time and money  shop the colors of the holiday at  hudson's bay but those iconic hudson's  bay images belie a pandemic ravaged year  and for the bay it got worse this week  the dispiriting picture of locked doors  at its store in coquitlam bc  after the landlord said the rent hadn't  been paid a hudson's bay north of  toronto had its lease terminated over  allegedly unpaid rent  and in quebec a judge ordered the bay to  pay outstanding rent at several stores  hudson's bay defends itself the ceo  telling global news in a statement  the burden posed by the pandemic should  be shared fairly by both landlords and  retailers  some landlords have agreed to make  reasonable accommodations  as for the others he says let the courts  decide i would describe  hudson's bay company as the canary and  the coal mine here for all of retailers  the impasse over rent exposes a deeper  problem for department stores  especially during covet 19. there's a  lot of space but fewer customers  the hudson's bay flagship store in  downtown toronto has an  estimated 850 000 square feet over  several floors  and the bay has a lot of big stores in  ontario alone  more than 30 locations and across the  country  almost 90 hudson's bay stores that's  millions of square feet  at a time when more and more people are  staying home and shopping online  the reality is in covet the shopping  centers are not bringing anywhere near  the traffic that's expected the bays had  to confront not just how people are  buying differently  but what they're buying people working  from home and sweatpants aren't looking  as much for high-end fashions  now a part of the bay's brand that was a  great attempt to reposition the company  and i think  if there had not been the cover this may  very well have worked  quite smoothly during the biggest  shopping season of the year the bay  hopes to keep its hardball  fight with landlords in the background  and to rely on canadian stars to catch  the eye of shoppers  to get through a cold covered christmas  two pairs of uggs  there's 13 of us still two  eric sorensen global news toronto  he was quite simply a soccer legend next  the tributes to diego maradona  unbelievable that was diego maradona  when he was at his best  he had no equal when he was on the pitch  he  was running the show the soccer legend  from argentina  died today at the age of 60. he had a  heart attack at his home in buenos aires  a week after surgery to remove a blood  clot from his brain  drug and alcohol problems engulfed  maradona later in life  but it is the dazzling way he played the  game that's being remembered  today maradona was one of the greatest  of all time  even other legends like pele  acknowledged that certainly  one day we'll kick a ball together in  heaven pele said today  maradona could thread his way through  the best players in the world  his mastery of the game control of the  ball and displays of utter joy  mesmerized fans  and in his homeland of argentina he was  revered  often referred to as the golden boy his  life was a rags to rich's story  he grew up in a slum got his first ball  when he was three  turned pro at 16.  famous for that hand of god goal in 1986  he led argentina to a world cup victory  the country's president has declared  three days of national mourning  you took us to the top of the world he  said you made us immensely happy  you were the greatest of all fellow  argentinian  leonel messi wrote today he has left us  but he isn't going anywhere because  diego  is eternal there has been so much loss  in 2020  and tonight we have the story of a  canadian hockey player who has  passed away he's not famous like  maradona he didn't even play many games  in the nhl  but his story is an important one he was  a trailblazer who opened doors for  generations that followed  here's mike armstrong he beat  just about the longest odds imaginable  fred zazakamoos was 19 years old when he  laced up his skates with the chicago  blackhawks  it was the original six era and he would  do battle with legends like  howe and richard only 125 hockey players  and six teams and i was one of them  it was a very short nhl career but a  long life  and an even longer legacy so saskamoos  is being remembered not for his 11 games  played  but for the fact he played them at all  theo fleury tweeted out that sasaka  moose helped indigenous hockey players  dream big while flyer's great reggie  leach says  as a boy he was inspired from the first  day he heard of sasaka moosa's success  but leech is also remembering the  hardships of his friend's life  sasaka moose would spend most of his  youth at a residential school in duck  lake saskatchewan  he was taken from his family when he was  just six years old with his brother  it was there that he would learn to play  organized hockey but it was also  there that he would live years of  mistreatment  i've been abused i see my brother you  just get abused  you know all that is  i don't think i'll forget it now in  hockey soccer moose would find  friendship with teammates but also  racism from opponents  and fans they were injuries he would  ignore but again  never forget despite all of that  sasaka moose would lead a life of  leadership he would inspire generations  through sports  and community service he spent 35 years  as a band counselor with the attacker  coupe cree nation  when i became a chief in 1980 then  i think it was one of the greatest  things that ever happened to me  to remember sasaka moose with numbers 11  games played in the nhl stands out  but there's also 120 grandchildren  and great-grandchildren another reason  he smiled  so much  and that is global national for this  wednesday i'm donna friesen  tonight's your canada is pender harbor  british columbia  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow take care of yourselves  bye-bye  you
 

